 "path": "arc-notes/task-54cad0a1.md",
 "content": "# Continual-ARC task pattern: rectangle with scattered markers

Observed in task-54cad0a1 (demos 1 and 2; test submission verified correct): an input contains a solid color-2 rectangle and isolated marker pixels (color 1 in the test, color 8 / 3 in demos). The output keeps the rectangle and markers, and extends color 2 in straight horizontal/vertical bands aligned with the rectangle. Markers act as directional cutoffs: on a rectangle row/column with an aligned external marker, extend on the side away from that marker; with no aligned marker, extensions can run to the grid edge. Preserve marker cells and do not overwrite them. The test confirmed this behavior for the horizontal rows (e.g. rectangle cols 9–15, markers on row 2 at col 6 => extend right; marker on row 3 at col 19 => extend left) and for vertical extensions. Some boundary/overlap details are not fully characterized; request demos if a future instance differs.

Demo shapes were solid rectangles with different dimensions and marker colors/locations, while the same ray-extension behavior held. Useful cue: do not treat isolated markers as a copied template; infer row/column projections from the filled rectangle, with the marker side determining the extension direction."
